theory.intro:
For logarithmic functions, the base must satisfy $a>0$ and $a\ne1$. The graphs of $y=a^{x}$ and $y=\log_{a}x$ are **inverses**, so each is the mirror image of the other in the line $y=x$. Reading one curve tells you about the other.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[width=7.5cm,height=7.5cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={-{Stealth}},xmin=-3.5,xmax=6,ymin=-3.5,ymax=6,xtick=\empty,ytick=\empty,clip=false,xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},samples=100]
\addplot[dashed,gray,domain=-3.5:6,samples=2] {x};
\addplot[thick,red!70!black,<->,domain=-3.5:2.58] {exp(x*ln(2))};
\addplot[thick,blue!70!black,<->,domain=0.088:6] {ln(x)/ln(2)};
\node[red!70!black] at (axis cs:-2.2,1.4) {$y=a^{x}$};
\node[gray] at (axis cs:5.2,3.9) {$y=x$};
\node[blue!70!black] at (axis cs:3.6,0.85) {$y=\log_{a}x$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

facts:
$y=\log_{a}x$ is an **increasing** curve when $a>1$.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[width=6.5cm,height=6.5cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={-{Stealth}},xmin=-1,xmax=9,ymin=-4,ymax=4,xtick=\empty,ytick=\empty,clip=false,xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},samples=100]
\addplot[thick,blue!70!black,<->,domain=0.09:8.9] {ln(x)/ln(2)};
\node[blue!70!black] at (axis cs:5.5,-2.2) {$a>1$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

$y=\log_{a}x$ is a **decreasing** curve when $0<a<1$; the only feature changed is the base, now less than $1$.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[width=6.5cm,height=6.5cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={-{Stealth}},xmin=-1,xmax=9,ymin=-4,ymax=4,xtick=\empty,ytick=\empty,clip=false,xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},samples=100]
\addplot[thick,blue!70!black,<->,domain=0.09:8.9] {ln(x)/ln(0.5)};
\node[blue!70!black] at (axis cs:5.5,2.2) {$0<a<1$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
For $y=3^x$:

| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| $y$ | $\frac19$ | $\frac13$ | $1$ | $3$ | $9$ |

Swap the coordinates to obtain points on $y=\log_3x$:

| $x$ | $\frac19$ | $\frac13$ | $1$ | $3$ | $9$ |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| $y$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |

Plot the points and draw smooth curves.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{logblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{expred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=7cm,axis equal image,axis lines=middle,axis line style={black,-{Stealth}},xmin=-2.5,xmax=10,ymin=-2.5,ymax=10,xtick={-2,1,3,6,9},ytick={-2,1,3,6,9},grid=major,grid style={gridgrey},scaled ticks=false,ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},clip=true]
\addplot[black,dashed,domain=-2.5:10,samples=2] {x};
\addplot[expred,thick,<->,domain=-2.3:2.08,samples=150] {exp(x*ln(3))};
\addplot[logblue,thick,<->,domain=0.08:9.8,samples=200] {ln(x)/ln(3)};
\addplot[expred,only marks,mark=*,mark size=1.5pt] coordinates {(-2,0.111111111) (-1,0.333333333) (0,1) (1,3) (2,9)};
\addplot[logblue,only marks,mark=*,mark size=1.5pt] coordinates {(0.111111111,-2) (0.333333333,-1) (1,0) (3,1) (9,2)};
\path (axis cs:4,8.5) node[text=expred,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] {$y=3^x$};
\path (axis cs:6.8,3.1) node[text=logblue,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] {$y=\log_3x$};
\path (axis cs:7.3,6.2) node[text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] {$y=x$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The graphs are reflections in $y=x$: each point $(p,q)$ becomes $(q,p)$. For example, $(2,9)$ becomes $(9,2)$.

The exponential graph has horizontal asymptote $y=0$. The logarithmic graph has vertical asymptote $x=0$.

Since $3>1$, the logarithmic graph is increasing. Every power of $3$ is positive, so $\log_3x$ is defined only for $x>0$. As $x$ approaches $0$ from the right, its output decreases without bound.

Write each input as a power of the relevant base. For example, $(\frac54)^{-2}=\frac{16}{25}$, so $\log_{\frac54}\frac{16}{25}=-2$. Also, $(\frac45)^2=\frac{16}{25}$, so $\log_{\frac45}\frac{16}{25}=2$.

| $x$ | $\frac{16}{25}$ | $\frac45$ | $1$ | $\frac54$ | $\frac{25}{16}$ |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| $\log_{\frac54}x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |
| $\log_{\frac45}x$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |

Plot both sets of points and draw smooth curves through them.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{logblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{logred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=7cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={black,-{Stealth}},xmin=-0.2,xmax=2.1,ymin=-3,ymax=3,xtick={0.5,1,1.5,2},ytick={-2,-1,1,2},grid=major,grid style={gridgrey},scaled ticks=false,ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},clip=true]
\addplot[logblue,thick,<->,domain=0.523:1.912,samples=150] {ln(x)/ln(1.25)};
\addplot[logred,thick,dashdotted,<->,domain=0.523:1.912,samples=150] {ln(x)/ln(0.8)};
\addplot[logblue,only marks,mark=*,mark size=1.5pt] coordinates {(0.64,-2) (0.8,-1) (1,0) (1.25,1) (1.5625,2)};
\addplot[logred,only marks,mark=square*,mark size=1.5pt] coordinates {(0.64,2) (0.8,1) (1,0) (1.25,-1) (1.5625,-2)};
\path (axis cs:1.3,2.65) node[text=logblue,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] {$y=\log_{5/4}x$};
\path (axis cs:1.3,-2.65) node[text=logred,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] {$y=\log_{4/5}x$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Since $\frac54>1$, $y=\log_{\frac54}x$ is increasing. Since $0<\frac45<1$, $y=\log_{\frac45}x$ is decreasing.

Both curves pass through $(1,0)$ and have vertical asymptote $x=0$. Their inputs must be positive; neither curve touches the $y$-axis.

